content.text:
Also in world.js `renderWorldScene` the old code removed `tile: T` — fine. But `renderWorld(ctx, W, H, view)` — `view.player.palette` is now PALETTES entry which drawPerson accepts. Good.

Now the `marker` drawing: old renderWorld drew a tap marker ring; new one doesn't draw it (removed). It's minor; keep out for now.

Next: NPCs need `walking` and wander behavior. Add simple wander in updateWorld for npcs with `wander: true`. Also `updatePrompt` uses `interactables()` — unchanged.

Now let me write the new hearthfall map in zones.js. Replace buildHearthfall and npcs list.

Let me now write zones.js hearthfall section. I'll replace from `function buildHearthfall()` to its return, and the npcs array.

Design coordinates (36 wide × 24 tall):

```
y=0-1: border '#'
y=2-3: elder hall roof at x=10-16 'h'; walls y=4-5 'H'; door x=13 y=5 'D'
y=4-5: walls with windows
```

Let me write it concretely using helpers house(), rect, outline, hline, vline, scatter, put.

```js
function buildHearthfall() {
  const m = make(36, 24, '.');
  const rng = mulberry32(7331);
  border(m, '#', 2);

  // grass variety
  scatter(m, '"', 26, rng, { x: 2, y: 2, w: 32, h: 20 });
  scatter(m, ',', 18, rng, { x: 2, y: 2, w: 32, h: 20 });
  scatter(m, '#', 10, rng, { x: 2, y: 2, w: 32, h: 20 });
  scatter(m, 'r', 6, rng, { x: 2, y: 2, w: 32, h: 20 });

  // houses
  house(m, 10, 2, 7, 4);     // elder hall (northwest of square)
  house(m, 2, 8, 7, 5);      // inn (west)
  house(m, 25, 6, 6, 5);     // forge (east)
  house(m, 20, 16, 5, 4);    // player's cottage (south-east)

  // windows in wall rows: house() fills walls with 'H'; windows are drawn randomly by tile fn — fine.

  // market stalls (east of square)
  rect(m, 22, 10, 4, 2, 'n');   // two stalls
  put(m, 21, 9, 'i');           // market sign
  put(m, 26, 9, 'i');

  // central stone square
  rect(m, 13, 8, 9, 6, '=');
  put(m, 14, 11, 'S');          // hearth crystal
  put(m, 12, 10, 'o'); put(m, 18, 10, 'o');  // festival braziers
  put(m, 15, 12, 'o'); put(m, 17, 13, 'o');
  put(m, 13, 7, 'L'); put(m, 21, 7, 'L'); put(m, 13, 14, 'L'); put(m, 21, 14, 'L');  // lanterns

  // notice board
  put(m, 20, 7, 'i');

  // roads
  vline(m, 17, 5, 21, '_', 1);            // main street north-south
  hline(m, 7, 13, 11, '_');               // west road to inn
  hline(m, 20, 27, 11, '_');              // east road
  vline(m, 7, 12, 12, '_');               // inn path
  vline(m, 27, 12, 12, '_');              // forge path
  hline(m, 15, 17, 8, '_');               // elder hall path
  vline(m, 10, 6, 6, '_');                // to elder hall

  // south gate + gatehouse
  rect(m, 13, 18, 9, 3, '_');             // gate approach
  rect(m, 14, 17, 7, 2, 'H');             // gatehouse wall? hmm overlapping
  put(m, 17, 22, 'P'); put(m, 16, 22, '_'); put(m, 18, 22, '_'); put(m, 17, 23, '_');
  rect(m, 13, 23, 9, 1, '#');             // gate border trees

  // pond (southwest)
  rect(m, 1, 17, 7, 4, '~');
  put(m, 0, 16, '~'); put(m, 8, 19, 's'); put(m, 7, 20, 's');
  put(m, 4, 16, 'w'); put(m, 3, 21, 'w');

  // garden (south of inn)
  outline(m, 9, 15, 5, 4, 'f');
  rect(m, 10, 16, 3, 2, ',');
  put(m, 11, 17, '"');

  // trees groves
  scatter(m, '#', 8, rng, { x: 28, y: 2, w: 6, h: 6 });
  scatter(m, 'T', 2, rng, { x: 29, y: 3, w: 4, h: 4 });
  scatter(m, '#', 8, rng, { x: 2, y: 11, w: 6, h: 8 });
  scatter(m, 'T', 1, rng, { x: 3, y: 13, w: 3, h: 4 });
  scatter(m, 't', 2, rng, { x: 2, y: 3, w: 2, h: 6 });

  return m;
}
```

Wait — old map used 'a' at (12,14)? `put(m, 12, 14, 'a');` — 'a' isn't a defined tile! Old drawGround had `else if (ch === 'a' || ...)`? Looking at old drawGround: `else if (ch === 'A' || ch === 'S' ...)` includes 'A' uppercase, not 'a'. So 'a' was treated as grass base with speckle? The condition `if (!['=', '_', 'b', ...].includes(ch)) base = shade(base, ...)` — 'a' not in list → shaded grass with speckles. OK 'a' was just grass variant. I won't use 'a'.

Gatehouse: I wrote `rect(m, 14, 17, 7, 2, 'H')` then road rect after overwrites part. Order matters: put gatehouse after roads or before. Let me place gatehouse wall at y=17-18, x=14-20, then gate approach road y=19-21 leading to portal at (17,22). Actually portal at y=22 row 22-23 is inside border? Border at y=22-23 is '#' (rect(m,0,22,36,2,'#')). Then put(m,17,22,'P') overwrites border with portal. Fine — old map did same (border 2 thick at bottom y=22-23, portal at 17,22).

Gatehouse: rect(m, 14, 17, 7, 2, 'H') — blocked wall with door at (17,17)? Let me put door at (17,18)? Hmm keep simple: gatehouse 'H' x14-20 y17-18, door 'D' at (17,18). Road '_' from y19 to y21 x15-19 leading to 'P' at (17,22). Good.

But main street vline x=17 from y5 to y21 — passes through gatehouse area y17-18 — overwritten by 'H'/'D'. Fine.

Also elder hall house() puts door at x + floor((w-1)/2) = 10+3 = 13, y+4-1 = y5 → (13,5) 'D'. Roof covers y2-3, walls y4-5. OK.

Inn: house(m,2,8,7,5): roof y8-9, walls y10-12, door at x=2+3=5, y=12 'D'.

Forge: house(m,25,6,6,5): roof y6-7, walls y8-10, door at x=25+2=27, y=10.

Player cottage: house(m,20,16,5,4): roof y16-17, walls y18-19, door at x=20+2=22, y=19.

Windows drawn randomly by tileHouseWall — every wall tile has ~chance? My tileHouseWall draws window only if hasWindow param — I pass false always. Houses look windowless. Let me add window param: house() helper doesn't support. Simpler: in buildHearthfall, after house(), put specific wall tiles as window variants? I don't have a window char. Alternative: modify tileHouseWall to randomly draw windows based on hash (some walls get windows). That gives houses windows procedurally. Good: in tileHouseWall, `const hasWindow = hash2(tx*11, ty*13) > .65;` — but same tile always same → consistent. Door tiles 'D' skip window. I'll add: if ch position is door skip. tileHouseWall doesn't know it's a door — 'D' drawn by tileHouseDoor separately. So wall tiles are 'H' only → windows fine.

Also layered rooftops: roof 'h' rows with tileHouseRoof — simple thatch. "layered rooftops" — could do two-tile-high roof with different shade — my roof has thatch rows + ridge. Acceptable.

Market stalls: 'n' tent drawn as stall with awning. Good.

Flowers '"' scattered — tileFlowers draws amber flowers. Good.

Now NPCs (must be on walkable tiles — not blocked):
- maro (17,10)? (17,10) is stone '='? Main street vline x=17 includes y10 → '_' actually! vline(m,17,5,21,'_') sets (17,10)='_'. Braziers at (12,10),(18,10),(15,12),(17,13) — 'o' blocked, so Maro can't be there. Place Maro at (17,11) stone path '=' (walkable) near crystal/brazier. 
- barda (26,12)? Forge walls y8-10 x25-30; (26,12) grass — walkable. But road hline y=11 x20-27 — (26,11)='_'. Put barda at (26,11) on road in front of forge.
- nessa (6,13)? Inn walls x2-8 y10-12; (6,13) grass walkable. Road vline x=7 y12-12. Put nessa (7,13)? That's path. (6,13) grass fine.
- yara (11,16)? Garden fence outline x9-13 y15-18; rect(m,10,16,3,2,',') → (10-12,16-17) tall grass. Yara at (11,15)? On fence 'f' blocked. Put yara at (11,18)? fence bottom y=18 blocked. Put yara at (14,16)? grass near garden. OK (14,16).
- pip (20,11)? square east road hline y11 x20-27 → (20,11)='_' walkable. Good, pip on plaza edge.
- oma (5,15)? grass west. (5,15) — anything? vline x=7. (5,15) grass. Good.
- rhen (17,19)? gate approach rect y18-20 x13-21 '_' → (17,19)='_' walkable. Good.

Markers: maro '!' until prologueDone; etc. Keep logic from refreshWorld.

Zone data:
- portals: (17,22) → emberfield (20,2)
- npcs as above with dialog ids existing (elder, smith, innkeeper, herbalist, child, seer, guard)
- chests: hf_chest1 (28,14), hf_chest2 (30,18) — check walkability around: (28,14) grass east of forge road? hline x20-27 y11; (28,14) grass walkable adjacent to chest? Chest blocked itself; player stands adjacent. Fine.
- crystals: hf_crystal (14,11) — map 'S' at (14,11). 
- braziers: hf_b1 (12,10), hf_b2 (18,10), hf_b3 (15,12), hf_b4 (17,13). Map has 'o' at those.
- signs: (20,7) notice; (11,17) garden — but (11,17) is tall grass ',' from garden rect — I put sign 'i' there overwriting. OK.

encounters: encRate 0 (safe village) but add possible festival-night encounter? Keep safe.

NPC wander: add wander:true to pip and maro maybe.

NPC palettes: color field used as accent. spriteClass for drawPerson role = npc.sprite. Roles in CHAR_PALS: hero, maro, barda, nessa, yara, pip, oma, rhen, kael, villager, child. NPC sprites: 'elder'→ not in CHAR_PALS → villager fallback. Hmm Maro should use 'maro' pal. sprite names: elder, smith, inn, herbalist, child, seer, guard. CHAR_PALS keys: maro, barda, nessa, yara, pip, oma, rhen, villager, child. So set npc.sprite = 'maro' etc.? But drawPerson role affects hair/hat: 'maro' → hair 'short', no hat. Elder needs cap. Let me add role mapping in render: role = npc.sprite; for sprite 'elder' use pal CHAR_PALS.maro and role 'elder'. I'll add explicit mapping in npcsWithPal:

```js
const roleMap = { elder:'maro', smith:'barda', inn:'nessa', herbalist:'yara', child:'pip', seer:'oma', guard:'rhen' };
const key = roleMap[n.sprite] || n.sprite;
```

But drawPerson's `style.hat` for 'guard' → helm; 'seer' → veil; good with role = mapped key. And role 'maro' → hair short — elder look? Add role 'maro' style: hair 'cap'? I'll handle in CHAR_PALS — maro hair '#a89080' grey. For the hat, give maro role a cap: in style: `hat: role === 'guard' ? 'helm' : role === 'seer' ? 'veil' : role === 'maro' ? 'cap' : null`. Edit art.js style hat line.

Also for hero, role '' → villager fallback palette; pass class-based cloth? I'll pass pants/boots; cloth from pal (PALETTES entry cloth). Palettes have cloth: '#5a3a2a' etc. OK acceptable: hero wears palette cloth. Accent = class color. Good enough; distinct silhouettes come from class colors + hair.

Hmm — but for player, I want recognizable hero silhouette. Default class vanguard: tunic cloth from palette[0] '#5a3a2a' (brown), pants darker, boots. Fine.

Now also `npcsWithPal` — I add `pal` property. drawPerson reads `npc.pal`. Good.

Also `npc.walking` for wander: set for wanderers.

Add wander logic in updateWorld: after NPC loop... simpler: in updateWorld dt loop, for each npc with wander, acc timer; if > 1200, try random adjacent walkable tile not occupied, set path. Let me add:

```js
if (!world.paused) {
  ...
  // npc wander
  world.wanderAcc = (world.wanderAcc || 0) + dt;
  if (world.wanderAcc > 1100 + Math.random() * 800) {
    world.wanderAcc = 0;
    for (const n of world.npcs) {
      if (!n.wander) continue;
      const [dx, dy] = Object.values(DIRS)[Math.floor(Math.random()*4)];
